WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.900
[Music]

00:00:30.066 --> 00:00:32.666
[Narrator:] This is an enemy.

00:00:32.666 --> 00:00:37.232
An enemy far more dangerous than any we've encountered in battle.

00:00:37.233 --> 00:00:42.466
An enemy that is stealthy and insidious, silent and deadly.

00:00:42.466 --> 00:00:49.999
An enemy that works in the dark, in the warm, moist atmosphere of the tissues inside the human body,

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:53.233
particularly in the lungs.

00:00:53.233 --> 00:00:58.433
[Music]

00:00:58.433 --> 00:01:01.233
particularly in the lungs.
Making this enemy even more vicious is the fact

00:01:01.233 --> 00:01:03.733
that it selects the majority of its victims

00:01:03.733 --> 00:01:17.599
from those who are in the most productive years of their lives, from 15 to 45.

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:23.666
And this enemy is tireless, unrelenting.

00:01:23.666 --> 00:01:31.066
This year in the United States it will kill one person every nine minutes.

00:01:31.066 --> 00:02:23.432
[Music]

00:02:23.433 --> 00:02:28.333
In the three years following Pearl Harbor this enemy we've been talking about,

00:02:28.333 --> 00:02:34.366
working quietly at home, took more lives,

00:02:34.366 --> 00:02:39.066
many more than were lost by all of our armed services

00:02:39.066 --> 00:02:42.066
on all of our battlegrounds.

00:02:42.066 --> 00:02:48.866
The tragedy is that this horrible loss of life is unnecessary.

00:02:48.866 --> 00:02:53.366
For this enemy, whose name is tubercule bacillus, can be conquered.

00:02:53.366 --> 00:03:01.366
Tuberculosis, the highly contagious disease that this microbe causes, is curable.

00:03:01.366 --> 00:03:05.599
[Older man in hat:] Well, why aren't we doing something about it?

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:11.966
[Woman at typewriter:] Yes, why don't we drive it out of America once and for all?

00:03:11.966 --> 00:03:15.999
[Man with mustache:] If it's curable, why don't we cure it?

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:20.800
[Narrator:] Well, as a matter of fact we are curing it, and we have been.

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:23.066
For example, forty years ago,

00:03:23.066 --> 00:03:31.099
tuberculosis was the leading cause of death in the United States.

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:34.000
Today, it is seventh in line.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:37.100
So you see we have made progress,

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:41.400
but we haven't made enough, until recently that is.

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:46.400
Why? Well, one of the most dangerous things about tuberculosis

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:52.266
is that people are frequently not aware of it until the disease is fairly well advanced,

00:03:52.266 --> 00:03:57.866
and it is in the early stages, often before there are any recognizable symptoms,

00:03:57.866 --> 00:04:00.999
that it can be treated most effectively.

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:04.800
So, the problem of curing tuberculosis is a matter of finding it,

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:08.666
and finding it hasn't been easy when most victims of the disease

00:04:08.666 --> 00:04:17.866
don't even suspect they have it until it has really taken hold.

00:04:17.866 --> 00:04:25.366
The most reliable and accurate way to detect tuberculosis is by means of an X-ray examination of the chest,

00:04:25.366 --> 00:04:28.166
but with this being the only sure method,

00:04:28.166 --> 00:04:32.799
it is not strange that so many cases have gone undiscovered.

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:36.400
For few of us, particularly if we're feeling all right,

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:41.033
ever think of dropping in at the hospital or a clinic for a chest X-ray,

00:04:41.033 --> 00:04:48.033
and yet if every man, woman, and child in the United States long ago had done just this,

00:04:48.033 --> 00:04:52.866
we might have tuberculosis licked right this minute.

00:04:52.866 --> 00:04:58.966
Well, today we are getting underway with an all-out, unrelenting determination.

00:04:58.966 --> 00:05:03.199
War has been declared on tuberculosis.

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:05.833
One of the newest and best weapons in this war

00:05:05.833 --> 00:05:09.199
is a type of X-ray equipment that is truly portable.

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:14.533
Using it, federal, state, county, and city health department and Tuberculosis Associations

00:05:14.533 --> 00:05:16.766
are today making it possible to bring the means

00:05:16.766 --> 00:05:22.932
for locating and stamping out tuberculosis literally to the doorstep of the nation.

00:05:22.933 --> 00:05:25.166
In places like large industrial plants,

00:05:25.166 --> 00:05:28.199
where a great many people can be examined at one time,

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:30.866
the equipment is set up on the premises.

00:05:30.866 --> 00:05:34.566
Affordable X-ray units will soon be coming to you here.

00:05:34.566 --> 00:05:38.799
To see what just happens, let's take a look at Eileen Brown, the stenographer.

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:44.766
We find her entertaining some girls at her home.

00:05:44.766 --> 00:05:48.366
[First woman on sofa:] Do you know anything about this X-ray business, Eileen?

00:05:48.366 --> 00:05:50.732
[Eileen:] I read about it coming home on the bus.

00:05:50.733 --> 00:05:52.933
It says one of those portable X-ray jobs will be stationed

00:05:52.933 --> 00:05:56.333
down by the firehouse all next week.

00:05:56.333 --> 00:05:58.099
[Second woman on sofa:] Are you going to have yours taken?

00:05:58.100 --> 00:06:02.433
[Eileen:] I guess so. Why not? It's free.

00:06:02.433 --> 00:06:06.099
And besides, I want to be sure I'm healthy.

00:06:06.100 --> 00:06:13.900
I'd hate to present Tom with a wife who is anything but a hundred percent perfect when he gets back.

00:06:13.900 --> 00:06:21.066
Come on, chums. Here's a snack that'll feed the body. Come and get it.

00:06:21.066 --> 00:06:32.766
[Second woman:] Mmm, wonderful. I'm starved. Thank you.

00:06:32.766 --> 00:06:37.299
[First woman:] Mmm. Gee, that was good. I'll probably be awake all night,

00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:42.133
but I'd love another cup of coffee.

00:06:42.133 --> 00:06:44.666
[Second woman:] You know, I'm kind of afraid.

00:06:44.666 --> 00:06:46.266
[Eileen:] Oh? What of?

00:06:46.266 --> 00:06:48.999
[Second woman:] That X-ray business. I'll bet it hurts.

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:53.266
[Eileen:] Why, that's nonsense. Haven't you ever had an X-ray at the dentist?

00:06:53.266 --> 00:06:56.032
They don't hurt.

00:06:56.033 --> 00:07:00.833
[First woman:] One of those units came to my husband's plant about a month ago and he had one made.

00:07:00.833 --> 00:07:04.899
He said there wasn't anything to it. It only took a few minutes.

00:07:04.900 --> 00:07:06.866
I'm worried about another part, though.

00:07:06.866 --> 00:07:08.999
[Second woman:] What's that?

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:12.000
[First woman:] Well, suppose you do have it.

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:14.400
Doesn't that mean that you'd have to quit work,

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:19.333
and go to a sanitorium, and maybe get operated on?

00:07:19.333 --> 00:07:23.266
Well, you know.

00:07:23.266 --> 00:07:32.999
[Eileen:] That's silly. It says here that if you do have it, and they let you know privately,

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:37.233
chances are you have a mild case that can be cured with rest.

00:07:37.233 --> 00:07:41.666
That doesn't sound very grim to me.

00:07:41.666 --> 00:07:47.632
And besides, if you had it, you'd want to be cured as soon as you could be, wouldn't you?

00:07:47.633 --> 00:07:50.866
And the earlier you discover you've got it,

00:07:50.866 --> 00:07:52.932
the quicker you can be cured.

00:07:52.933 --> 00:07:55.733
That sounds like sense to me.

00:07:55.733 --> 00:07:59.933
Besides, what if you do kid yourself and don't get it done?

00:07:59.933 --> 00:08:05.033
You don't expect it to go away by pretending it isn't there, do you?

00:08:05.033 --> 00:08:10.099
[Narrator:] Eileen Brown is wise to realize that she's doing herself and others a favor

00:08:10.100 --> 00:08:13.766
by taking advantage of this X-ray service.

00:08:13.766 --> 00:08:17.266
Let's watch as she goes through the procedure.

00:08:17.266 --> 00:08:20.299
First, she supplies certain information about herself:

00:08:20.300 --> 00:08:25.466
her name, address, age, sex, marital status and so on.

00:08:25.466 --> 00:08:28.099
Then in a private dressing booth,

00:08:28.100 --> 00:08:31.666
she changes into a cape that has been given her for this purpose.

00:08:31.666 --> 00:08:38.599
Now we're ready for the X-ray.

00:08:38.600 --> 00:08:43.900
[X-ray tech:] Now we'll measure your chest.

00:08:43.900 --> 00:08:53.833
[Nurse:] You can stand right over here, please.

00:08:53.833 --> 00:09:09.633
Stand straight with your shoulders turned forward. Well that's fine. Now don't move.

00:09:09.633 --> 00:09:11.099
All right.

00:09:11.100 --> 00:09:17.766
[X-ray tech:] Take a deep breath. Hold it. Now relax.

00:09:17.766 --> 00:09:19.399
[Nurse:] That's all, easy, wasn't it?

00:09:19.400 --> 00:09:23.100
[Eileen:] It certainly was. That's as simple as having a snapshot taken.

00:09:23.100 --> 00:09:26.833
[Nurse:] It's simpler. You don't have to worry about the sun getting in your eyes.

00:09:26.833 --> 00:09:28.933
[Giggles]

00:09:28.933 --> 00:09:33.933
[Narrator:] Some time later the films are developed 
and then examined by a specialist.

00:09:33.933 --> 00:09:39.866
Many people who do not have tuberculosis have found this free X-ray examination worthwhile,

00:09:39.866 --> 00:09:43.432
because it has revealed other chest and heart conditions

00:09:43.433 --> 00:09:47.299
that warranted investigation by a physician.

00:09:47.300 --> 00:09:50.466
Of the millions of people already examined,

00:09:50.466 --> 00:09:53.466
only about one and one half percent were found to have any tuberculosis,

00:09:55.600 --> 00:10:02.833
but it is by finding this small percentage of apparently healthy people, and curing them,

00:10:02.833 --> 00:10:12.066
that we're going to control tuberculosis and finally eradicate it.

00:10:12.066 --> 00:10:18.699
Eradicate it so the citizens of a strong America will be able to enjoy life to the fullest

00:10:18.700 --> 00:10:26.300
and will be able to pass on the priceless legacy of health to the generations to come.

00:10:26.300 --> 00:10:33.133
Think a moment, don't you think it is worthwhile for you to have your chest X-rayed?

00:10:33.140 --> 00:10:44.260
[Music]

